anguage (SHACL) is now a W3C Recommendation", "url": "https://www.w3.org/news/2017/shapes-constraint-language-shacl-is-now-a-w3c-recommendation/", "publisher": "W3C News", "date": "2017"}, {"title": "SHACL 1.2 Core (Working Draft)", "url": "https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl-core/", "publisher": "W3C Data Shapes Working Group", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "SHACL 1.2 Node Expressions (First Public Working Draft)", "url": "https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-shacl12-node-expr-20260108/", "publisher": "W3C", "date": "8 Jan 2026"}, {"title": "SHACL Validation in the Presence of Ontologies: Semantics and Rewriting Techniques", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12286", "publisher": "Oudshoorn, Ortiz and Šimkus, TU Wien (arXiv)", "date": "2025"}]}-->
<p>A common pattern in knowledge graph projects: the team spends months on a careful ontology, loads data from a dozen sources, and then discovers that a third of the customer nodes have no country, some products have two prices and a few suppliers are typed as people. Nothing in the ontology stopped it, because that isn't what an ontology does.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>OWL and SHACL do different jobs</h2>
<p>OWL reasons under an open-world assumption: a missing fact is unknown, not false, and the language is built to infer new facts from the ones you have. SHACL validates the data graph as it stands, so a missing required property is a violation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The W3C Recommendation describes this as checking "data graphs" against conditions in "shapes graphs"<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th></th><th>OWL</th><th>SHACL</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Question it answers</td><td>What follows from what we know?</td><td>Is this data acceptable?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Missing value</td><td>Unknown</td><td>A violation, if the shape requires it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Output</td><td>Inferred facts</td><td>A validation report</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical owner</td><td>Ontology and domain experts</td><td>Data engineering and data quality</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Combining the two is not trivial. Research from TU Wien describes the semantic gap between OWL reasoning and SHACL validation as a major challenge, and notes conflicts, for example between reasoning and closed shapes<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. In practice I keep them in separate stages and decide explicitly whether validation runs before or after inference.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Write shapes for the entities that matter first.</strong> Customer, product, patient, supplier: whatever your use cases depend on. Cardinality, datatypes, allowed values and required relationships cover most real problems.</li>
<li><strong>Validate at the boundary.</strong> Run SHACL on each source's data before it joins the main graph, so bad data is quarantined with a report rather than merged.</li>
<li><strong>Use severities deliberately.</strong> Not every failure should block a load. Separate hard violations from warnings, and route warnings to the data owner instead of the pipeline.</li>
<li><strong>Decide where inference sits.</strong> If you validate after reasoning, test that inferred facts don't create spurious violations, particularly with closed shapes<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Keep shapes in version control</strong> next to the ontology, reviewed like code and changed through the same process. A shape change can reject yesterday's valid data.</li>
<li><strong>Track validation results over time.</strong> Violation counts per source and per shape are a data quality metric the business can understand.</li>
<li><strong>Validate what AI writes.</strong> When an LLM extracts entities into the graph, as in <a href="/insights/graphrag-when-it-beats-vector-rag/">GraphRAG</a>, run its output through the same shapes. Extraction errors are cheap to catch at the door and expensive to find later.</li>
<li><strong>Pair with entity resolution.</strong> Shapes catch malformed records; they don't catch two records for the same customer. That needs <a href="/insights/entity-resolution-knowledge-graphs/">entity resolution</a>.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p>The rule I give teams: no data enters the shared graph without passing its shapes. The ontology says what the world looks like; the shapes are the contract every source has to meet.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where the standard is going</h2>
<p>SHACL became a W3C Recommendation in July 2017, with Core constraints and SPARQL-based extensions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. The W3C Data Shapes Working Group is now developing SHACL 1.2, with 2026 Working Drafts for Core and for Node Expressions among others<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. They are drafts and may change, so I build on the 2017 Recommendation and track 1.2 for features worth adopting once they settle.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why this matters for AI</h2>
<p>Knowledge graphs are increasingly the grounding layer for LLM applications. A graph that answers confidently with malformed or contradictory data is worse than no graph, because the model will present it fluently. Validation is what makes a graph trustworthy enough to ground AI, and it fits the layered model in <a href="/insights/ontology-vs-knowledge-graph-vs-semantic-layer/">ontology vs knowledge graph vs semantic layer</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I can help</h2>
<p>I design ontologies, shapes and validation pipelines for enterprise knowledge graphs, so they stay trustworthy as sources and AI extraction grow. See <a href="/ontology-knowledge-graph/">ontology and knowledge graphs</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
